<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST10728"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:bmodule_244@hotmail.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Bmodule<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Bmodule<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, March 06, 2005 - 5:49 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I picked up on the date also, except I noticed that 1-74, could also be a reference to the Michael Williams shooting &#40;notice that no specific date was given, but rather the entire month is referenced&#41;. Ever since there has been a strong Coleman connection re-established I find it hard to overlook this as being connected in some way. <BR> <BR>In march 2004 BTK Comes back, also Raders cousin, Dean Newton Raider, is relaeased from prison. There are allegations that he had raped his own daughter, which if true, would put him in the same sub-human sub-strata as Dennis. <BR> <BR>Michael Williams was a supervisor of assemby and Dennis Rader was an assembly worker. I can&#39;t imagine Rayder working in ANY factory and NOT having some animosity for an immediate supervisor whose father was a big wheel in the company. Anyone as self focused as Rayder, although not necessarily outwardly, would privately, never be able to stand on an assembly line for 8 hours working and not resent the &#34;big wig&#39;s son&#34; acting like a big shot. <BR> <BR>When the shooting occured, Williams father was quick to point out, even insistant, that the incident was a failed robbery attempt, however, if you are going to rob someone you force the door open as soon as it&#39;s unlatched, you don&#39;t just stand there with a shotgun waiting to be invited in. <BR> <BR>A year earlier Williams reported a burglary during which a radio and a rifle were the only things that the burglar found worth taking. That was either a pretty picky burgar, or Williams didn&#39;t have much worth stealing, which make me wonder why he was targeted, just one year later, for a strong arm robbery which it was determined would require two men and at least one shotgun to pull off. <BR> <BR>If Rader was involved, the brutality of the Otero murder may have been to warn the Williams&#39; to keep their mouths shut.  <BR> <BR>Probably when the robbers shot him through the door, the combination of the buckshot pattern being spread and the shrapnel from the door hitting the body made Williams a bloody mess. That, and the fact that very few people survive a shotgun blast in the abdomen from near point-blank range, probably convinced the intruders that he was dead or dying. <BR> <BR>When they found out from the news that he was alive there may have been a phone call telling the Williams&#39; to keep quiet. Then, to be sure, the Otero family, another Coleman worker who Rader may have had a problem that both of the Williams&#39; would know about, is tortured and murdered along with her family, her daughter brutalized, and her son tied to a chair with bags on his head &#40;was there any other evidence about the son that the police suppressed, since they didn&#39;t seem to have any problem with describing how Josephine was left hanging half naked and bound. Or the part of the letter from BTK describing her &#34;pleading eyes&#34; as he watched her hang&#41;. <BR> <BR>After the Williams&#39; realized that the murders would continue, they would be reluctant to come forward with information, both from the fear of retribution and from the awkwardness of explaining how they were cowered in the first place. <BR> <BR>Hell, if Dean is still out there, they probably don&#39;t feel safe , even now. <BR> <BR> <BR>This is all simply conjecture..................of course.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
