<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11897"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:bmodule_244@hotmail.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Bmodule<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Bmodule<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, March 20, 2005 - 5:23 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Bestbud <BR> <BR>Please DO find the link to the solution. It&#39;s REAL easy to blow these things off as child&#39;s games because...well... they&#39;re child&#39;s games. <BR> <BR>This particular one, though, is short and the letters which are NOT present are the ones that make up the traditional BTK words and themes. That has led me to believe that it is a straight forward &#40;no offset&#41; jumble that he took a lot of time to put together so it would not be misconstrued. <BR> <BR>It occurred to me recently as someone mentioned their theory that Rader&#39;s plan was to &#34;unfinish the BTK story&#34; by reappearing for a year which would culminate in another &#40;much more sensational&#41; murder makes me wonder about my, and everyone else&#39;s, immediate assumption about the &#39;1-74&#39; refering to the start of his puplic personna as BTK.  <BR> <BR>When his Air Force record affirmed he did indeed work as a radio tech, it also affirmed that he would have worked along side Pschyc-Ops personnel and would have picked up on some of their tricks. <BR> <BR>Trick #1 in propoganda: Provide false premise that you want the enemy to take as fact. Trick #2: plant clues to support the premise. What makes it effective is that the enemy has to work to figure out it out, so they feel rewarded when they connect the dots. [Hard Work=Reward] is how the world is supposed to work, so they feel they are deserving of the truth, therefore, what you have made them work for, which all comes together in logical sequence, must be the truth, therefore, correct. <BR> <BR>Suppose now, if BTK was planning another horrific crime, that he had provided the name or address  &#40;or both&#41; of the intended victim to the authorities a full year in advance.  <BR> <BR>That would be classic BTK. It would also completely undermine the faith of the public in the WPD&#39;s investigative ability &#40;even without their help&#41;.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 11897-->
<!--Post: 11976--><!--Time: 1111470929-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11976"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Pax56<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Pax56<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, March 21, 2005 - 9:55 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Sidewalker - good thought.  They could be solution hints, like the &#34;440&#34; was.  If so, such a solution on a known BTK puzzle might de-poser-ize the 440 and RIAC posts.  I&#39;m still scratching my head, though. <BR> <BR>Bmod - still laughing on broken ribs a week after the &#34;three dude&#34; post.  It will serve us right if the final message is something equally profound and revealing. <BR> <BR>V - lol!  My computer &#40;&#34;mother&#34;&#41; is unable to solve it.  However, the encrypted form appears to be some kind of a warning to Rader - something about &#34;soap&#34; and prison.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
